</section>
<section aria-labelledby="layout-h">
  <h2 id="layout-h" class="type-h2">Layout and spacing</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Case study hero layout by breakpoint, scrollable">
    <table class="docs-table type-small">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Breakpoint and layout</th><th scope="col">Arrangement</th><th scope="col">Band padding above and below</th><th scope="col">Gap between text and image</th><th scope="col">Gap between text groups</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Mobile</th><td>Text, then the image full width</td><td>32px</td><td>24px</td><td>32px</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tablet (768px up)</th><td>Text, then the image full width</td><td>48px</td><td>32px</td><td>40px</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Desktop (1024px up), below</th><td>12 columns: text 8 columns wide, then the image across all 12</td><td>64px</td><td>48px</td><td>40px</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Desktop, beside</th><td>12 columns: text in columns 1 to 5, image in columns 6 to 12, text stacked from the top</td><td>64px</td><td>24px (column gap)</td><td>40px</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The text groups are the eyebrow and title, the subtitle, and the buttons. The sides use the page padding, so the text lines up with the header. The back row is 8px above and below on mobile and 16px from 768px up. The back button is pulled left by 12px so its arrow lines up with the grid and not with the button's own inset. The eyebrow and title are 16px apart. The buttons stack at full width on mobile and sit in a row from 768px up, 16px apart.</p>
  <p>The two settings are classes on the <code>cs-hero</code> element. Add <code>cs-hero--band</code> for the band tone and <code>cs-hero--beside</code> for the beside layout. With neither class you get the plain tone with the image below. On tablet and mobile <code>cs-hero--beside</code> changes nothing.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="color-h">
  <h2 id="color-h" class="type-h2">Color</h2>
  <p><strong>Plain tone.</strong> The hero sits on <code>surface/neutral/base</code>. The eyebrow is <code>text/neutral/secondary</code>, the title is <code>text/brand/primary</code> and the subtitle is <code>text/neutral/body</code>. There is no band and no matte.</p>
  <p><strong>Band tone.</strong> The back row and the text sit on <code>surface/brand/primary</code>, the same in Light and Dark. The eyebrow is <code>text/on-brand/secondary</code>, the title is <code>text/on-brand/primary</code> (white) and the subtitle is <code>text/on-brand/body</code>. The back link is white (<code>text/on-brand/primary</code>, with <code>icon/on-brand/default</code> for the arrow) and sits inside the band. The image sits in a white matte, <code>surface/on-brand/matte</code>: 16px of padding on desktop and tablet, 12px on mobile. The image stays 16:9 inside it, so a screenshot with any background reads cleanly.</p>
  <p>Buttons in the hero actions use the on-brand secondary button when the hero is on the band. The title is white on the band and blue on the page. Neither uses yellow.</p>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="use-h">
  <h2 id="use-h" class="type-h2">Using the case study hero</h2>
  <div class="rules">
    <div class="rule"><h3 class="type-h3">Do</h3><ul>
      <li>Use one at the top of a case study, with the page's one <code>h1</code> as the title.</li>
      <li>Give the image a text alternative that says what it shows.</li>
      <li>Put the width and height on the image so the page does not jump while it loads.</li>
      <li>Use the eyebrow for the type of project and the role.</li>
      <li>Choose the tone and layout for each case study. The band is a deliberate choice, not a default.</li>
    </ul></div>
    <div class="rule"><h3 class="type-h3">Don't</h3><ul>
      <li>Use the buttons for links inside the site. They are secondary buttons that open a new tab.</li>
      <li>Use an image that is not 16:9. The hero crops it to 16:9, so the edges can be lost.</li>
      <li>Leave the alt text empty. The image carries content.</li>
      <li>Put a matte on the plain tone. The matte belongs to the band.</li>
      <li>Lazy-load the hero image. Give it <code>fetchpriority="high"</code>.</li>
    </ul></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="a11y-h">
  <h2 id="a11y-h" class="type-h2">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The hero is a <code>&lt;header&gt;</code> with one <code>h1</code>.</li>
    <li>Each button that opens a new tab says so to a screen reader with the hidden text "(opens in new tab)", and shows the open-in-new icon.</li>
    <li>The back button is a link with a visible label, so the arrow is not the only signal. On the band it is white on blue (9.62:1).</li>
    <li>On the band, the title, eyebrow, subtitle and back link all clear 4.5:1 against the blue.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
